#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b0dbd2f9-8fef-5af8-b4ce-a371e65c6441" :question/math-academy-id "e-21830" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x^3 - x + 5\\cos x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-\\pi,\\pi).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in(-\\pi,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/9d/9d4ae844370b06d82ce892e052ecd1c114519a6bb31e153e3bcb10c2c4806d56.svg)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "43229a59-0b01-55ff-8b10-b1238360d277" :question/math-academy-id "e-21831" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x).$ \n\n![](images/4f/4f5284fb6fed9d16cafa85a546e65db1b9ebf5a5211d870b24dfe87bb1ccb49b.svg)" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-6x-2$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi (-6x-2) \\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-6x-2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(-6x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{6}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-6\\pi -2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{6}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0-\\frac{6}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim (-2-3\\pi) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}} \n\\cdot \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\cos(nx)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166574 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "c8b4459b-e0ed-56cc-ab06-5be09baa7363" :question/math-academy-id "e-21833" :question/problem "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_0.$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "593a5576-438c-5a88-b1ab-4d454dbfe49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312688} {:answer/id #uuid "8b20e797-c5ce-5f0c-aa2b-9769c0a817b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313082} {:answer/id #uuid "914eefbb-7b51-5a10-b982-aac4cf2c32e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{2}+2x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313133} {:answer/id #uuid "ce996b3c-559e-5ad2-a307-7344e79bfbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313589} {:answer/id #uuid "db8d13f9-28a0-54cd-893d-2c8b3f144b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{2}+2|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b20e797-c5ce-5f0c-aa2b-9769c0a817b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313082} :answer-field/id #uuid "597899e8-1ef3-5f83-bfcf-33d3f5ac1fde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcf50127-6a15-5bfa-b137-f8e9738bae25" :question/math-academy-id "q-340794" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}-2(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ab0d3f-c0ec-5341-aee5-a68517e3ea54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420172} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef189ae-b8a9-5aad-a7b1-aabed08f2956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420174} {:answer/id #uuid "c84c6a75-c353-5602-bb33-16dd82b9c8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420179} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b3c15-c486-552b-8364-93f50ed30f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c84c6a75-c353-5602-bb33-16dd82b9c8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420179} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eb4b0f3-7bad-579d-a2a9-a9b4d07fac23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d92551-833d-5ca4-a56b-7ab1b74f7b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420175} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7d2516-8e80-5537-9f0b-2046239632d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420178} {:answer/id #uuid "e49f1d50-aff3-5106-a1bc-06b9c13d3402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420180} {:answer/id #uuid "edfa8848-8f15-59cc-bc41-52226333544c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e49f1d50-aff3-5106-a1bc-06b9c13d3402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420180} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc993b7-9a19-50f4-ac56-028c499dcaa2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2a82489-ea8c-529e-9b98-bcb721239fc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-340812" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=2x+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(3+\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2x+3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+3\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{2}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{2}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{2}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(3+\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "900c2584-40c5-5fbf-84a9-3292a82b4f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({\\pi }^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313126} {:answer/id #uuid "21b1871f-2843-5201-8ffe-93f44e6e1f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21b1871f-2843-5201-8ffe-93f44e6e1f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549384} :answer-field/id #uuid "060c12eb-87ed-5758-947e-4bd5ff6302f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d95b499-c990-5e2e-96ad-0c45f0c2fce8" :question/math-academy-id "q-340814" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[{x}^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }({\\pi }^{3}+2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2({\\pi }^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af28b770-ae88-5a31-80df-f0b664e52aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{3}+|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313341} {:answer/id #uuid "ba559227-5f6d-5a3f-a5b7-d8f6c9ff4b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313427} {:answer/id #uuid "d298aadc-ec0a-59b6-a1c4-a181506abe7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{3}+x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313625} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5212cb-169d-5ee0-8c4b-ef27a6dc9f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313877} {:answer/id #uuid "f46f04ee-46bb-52c3-83db-8477ccd31b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f46f04ee-46bb-52c3-83db-8477ccd31b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313930} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a18eef6-ce6b-5df3-bf8d-2434bbf8e335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da2cbfde-d64b-5d60-ab62-7e1044f8ad53" :question/math-academy-id "q-341240" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c3e4b13-f674-524e-998b-ca67fbac5bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-x}-|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549470} {:answer/id #uuid "53f8248e-b61e-57c4-9472-cf097523c0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-x}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549485} {:answer/id #uuid "8a035a53-368d-5ef3-a187-6c5da17a9adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{x}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549577} {:answer/id #uuid "a68a74b2-3252-5129-925b-06ed8785be3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|3{e}^{x}-x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549631} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf6290f-8f7d-5951-80e5-580a05d0949d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{x}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53f8248e-b61e-57c4-9472-cf097523c0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-x}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549485} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d248267-7b72-5ad1-a04c-f3637b43ad5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c67b1cfd-40e2-5a99-9d17-b7d5268f4dcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-341248" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=3{e}^{x}+x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{(-x)}+(-x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15bb385e-7f13-50d3-937e-b59ebadbf90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9e^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15bb385e-7f13-50d3-937e-b59ebadbf90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9e^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420173} :answer-field/id #uuid "49fca85c-c10d-570a-9fbd-829efc317583" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75e9cee8-821a-5456-9b71-a9061b7a8387" :question/math-academy-id "q-341252" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is the value of $f(-3)?$\n\n$f(-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}{e}^{(-3)^{2}}=9{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1cad84c-acf8-5668-92f8-e74a909e2dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1cad84c-acf8-5668-92f8-e74a909e2dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549681} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c5cf45-afa5-52d9-bf6d-70e8f10c09ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7016461-6868-574f-be42-632e56b55fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-341253" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x{e}^{x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is the value of $f(-2)?$\n\n$f(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-(-2){e}^{-(-2)}=2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e18a8cac-f51a-57be-9cc2-c525c3637b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372766} {:answer/id #uuid "781ea28a-5250-545f-b426-c20605d57acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(1-(-1)^{n})}{4\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372767} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc8ee33-a5d4-5c99-83fe-09ac643b6da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372768} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd6f489-a5d5-5355-81af-26e059a9b049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372769} {:answer/id #uuid "b70bf7b4-8c32-581d-b0ef-1705f7404c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bd6f489-a5d5-5355-81af-26e059a9b049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372769} :answer-field/id #uuid "a167a142-5a35-5de0-bf3c-bfdf6ad5f747" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac7731e8-df1e-5709-a827-9379c5eafbc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-341257" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-4x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-4x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{8}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{8(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aac0952-6642-50f4-bcba-5e0871212b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372773} {:answer/id #uuid "f61d5b67-1fbd-564c-84a2-e9f823d7bdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372774} {:answer/id #uuid "4957294e-b0c3-50f1-8722-39a9052d876a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372775} {:answer/id #uuid "51321fa7-40b6-5ae9-a202-70e56f448715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372776} {:answer/id #uuid "10553804-d22d-5423-85aa-8a4af993892b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10553804-d22d-5423-85aa-8a4af993892b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372777} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff2bab86-e0ff-5867-a654-27cbff74e3c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "154b5b2f-2b2c-5a08-9108-52d945d93470" :question/math-academy-id "q-341260" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-8x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-8x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{16}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{16(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{16(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b55494-ac2b-59d3-bb0c-453e8c568926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549408} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd2ef81-c957-504d-a56d-8a2440f1685a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549501} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4fbc5f-b76c-5ff7-bf3b-9523ba3c19b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549639} {:answer/id #uuid "eae7207f-15cc-547a-b08d-546f2343cef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29b55494-ac2b-59d3-bb0c-453e8c568926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549408} :answer-field/id #uuid "901c601c-f36e-56ff-8079-21f7d8654a35" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549867} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "056c1313-4dba-5537-a415-25f647daba6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549324} {:answer/id #uuid "0eff6f24-e788-53dc-aa43-0b1981e833d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549339} {:answer/id #uuid "58ac06ce-36f1-5449-b206-87a2a6fdd1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549494} {:answer/id #uuid "f025807f-1fdc-5d45-89ab-f5216051c571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "056c1313-4dba-5537-a415-25f647daba6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549324} :answer-field/id #uuid "98620a41-f0fa-569b-a3ec-5642c8206fa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55d2ffed-9ece-55ed-baea-5ef5a75af131" :question/math-academy-id "q-341270" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=4x-1$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(-1+2\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4x-1$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4x-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x-1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(4x-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((4\\pi -1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{4}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{4}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(-1+2\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53619ed7-f716-5872-bb91-4731ed352f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549482} {:answer/id #uuid "af2db65f-09c0-5208-935f-ed4720f1faa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549643} {:answer/id #uuid "bd23cd8e-42fd-5b65-a59e-dd4b56e28318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549672} {:answer/id #uuid "dca6add2-e954-506c-a341-17bf48c25508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dca6add2-e954-506c-a341-17bf48c25508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549724} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8649ec8-1d2d-592d-8063-88c05a6c45a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549905} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "576ea93a-944b-514c-b440-bd611ca2b89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549493} {:answer/id #uuid "ae359aa2-bf84-57b5-a9b5-a73878b31559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549642} {:answer/id #uuid "b86447de-2c58-5341-ad44-f2f7823d34e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549662} {:answer/id #uuid "e939c31e-32f1-563a-b202-962cdcdaa980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae359aa2-bf84-57b5-a9b5-a73878b31559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549642} :answer-field/id #uuid "e947fd84-1d63-5d10-af30-150e8c1aaa94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3fb491ae-d461-5efd-a743-cfec479bf958" :question/math-academy-id "q-341272" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=7x-4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(-4+\\frac{7\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=7x-4$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(7x-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=7x-4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=7 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(7x-4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{7}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((7\\pi -4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{7}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{7}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{14((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(-4+\\frac{7\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{14((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a81ffa-dc5e-51a3-a7da-856e67b88f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{5\\pi^{2}}{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a81ffa-dc5e-51a3-a7da-856e67b88f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{5\\pi^{2}}{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549535} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdceacd7-e832-59cd-b2a8-3585e4638dba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c123c90-9e3c-534f-a040-61cb255aa0e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-341274" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=5{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=5{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(5{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{3}}{3}+\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19318467-73e5-540d-b86c-017adc969dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312153} {:answer/id #uuid "07aa64d0-a645-5949-a1b8-1717374a9409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{5\\pi^{2}}{3}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07aa64d0-a645-5949-a1b8-1717374a9409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{5\\pi^{2}}{3}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549325} :answer-field/id #uuid "709ccfa5-c570-5e8b-84e0-9153e5971d2e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c31042af-d0b1-56e0-8fc6-7b7b6e402e06" :question/math-academy-id "q-341276" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=5{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=5{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(5{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{3}}{3}+2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50462a04-0887-512d-8b96-cd650e92389f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50462a04-0887-512d-8b96-cd650e92389f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549477} :answer-field/id #uuid "989a790f-36d6-537f-bac0-75e070b26f48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6da2a50a-0f62-59bb-a9db-a784f084c0f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-341277" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(6{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[2{x}^{3}+x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(2{\\pi }^{3}+\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2(2{\\pi }^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70a30cec-1c74-58b6-a352-b55dbaad5ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312860} {:answer/id #uuid "6291bc20-b33b-51a2-81f3-8c4774fb834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6291bc20-b33b-51a2-81f3-8c4774fb834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420177} :answer-field/id #uuid "850f065e-3ad8-58f4-8d41-45b829bfc32b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1a98a94-1685-5290-8055-415073e9036d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341285" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
